pages=[]
def add(title, *paras, table=None, figure=False, note=None): pages.append((title,list(paras),table,figure,note))
add('CHAPTER 1 INTRODUCTION','Chronic conditions are commonly managed through repeated clinical measurements, structured review and patient participation. A software system can organize these inputs and present a consistent summary, but it must not convert a screening estimate into a diagnosis. TwinHealth AI is designed as an academic clinical decision-support prototype that separates model output from clinical confirmation. Its role is to support workflow, traceable assessment and discussion with a qualified clinician.','The project combines five disease-specific risk pipelines with a clinician-facing intake form, results page, care-plan draft and patient activity view. Inputs are checked for completeness before a result is shown. A missing or unsuitable input results in an insufficient-data status rather than a fabricated probability. This reduces the risk of presenting false certainty in a demonstration system.',figure=True)
add('1.1 PROBLEM STATEMENT','Patients with cardiometabolic and chronic-disease risk may have multiple relevant measurements dispersed across records, forms or laboratory reports. A clinician needs a compact way to capture values, identify unavailable evidence, view relevant risk estimates and decide whether further testing or referral is appropriate. Patients also benefit from a clear plan whose completion can be recorded without claiming that the software replaces clinical care.','The problem addressed in this project is therefore the design of a local-first web workflow that integrates structured intake, model-specific inference, explainable risk communication and activity tracking. The intended outcome is a reviewable report and care-plan draft, not an autonomous medical recommendation.',table=(['Issue','Project response'],[['Fragmented assessment data','Structured clinical intake and stored assessment record'],['Incomplete measurements','Explicit insufficient-data status'],['Opaque risk output','Factors and clear risk labels'],['Low plan visibility','Patient checklist and clinician monitoring'],['Unsafe interpretation','Visible clinical-review and diagnosis disclaimer']],[5,10]))
add('1.2 OBJECTIVES','The primary objective is to build a web application that produces disease-specific risk estimates from structured inputs and presents those estimates in a clinician-oriented workflow. The application must preserve an explicit boundary between predictive output and diagnosis.','Additional objectives are to retain assessment records locally, support patient and clinician roles, allow a clinician to prepare a care-plan draft, track completed activities and provide testable routes for the principal screens and services.',table=(['Objective','Acceptance evidence'],[['Capture relevant inputs','Form validation and saved assessment'],['Run five risk pipelines','Disease result objects returned'],['Explain output','Risk label and contributing factors displayed'],['Support activity tracking','Completion event persisted and visible'],['Preserve safety boundary','Risk-not-diagnosis wording on results']],[7,8]))
add('1.3 SCOPE OF THE PROJECT','The project scope includes a local FastAPI backend, browser-based frontend pages, locally stored model artifacts, JSON persistence and test coverage for core flows. Supported risk areas are type 2 diabetes, heart disease, chronic kidney disease, liver disease and stroke.','The scope excludes real-patient deployment, emergency triage, prescription generation, autonomous treatment decisions, regulated clinical validation and production identity assurance. The academic prototype must use only authorised or de-identified data during demonstrations.')
add('1.4 PROJECT CONTRIBUTIONS','The project contribution is an integrated academic prototype rather than a new diagnostic algorithm. It demonstrates how model-specific feature handling, a strict sufficiency check and a clinician review flow can be joined in one application. It also demonstrates local persistence for an assessment and a patient-facing checklist that can be reviewed through a clinician dashboard.','A second contribution is safety-aware presentation. The system can state that a risk estimate is elevated, moderate or low based on configured thresholds, but it should always direct the clinician or user to confirmatory clinical testing and professional judgement.')
add('1.5 ORGANISATION OF THE REPORT','Chapter 1 introduces the problem, objectives and scope. Chapter 2 reviews relevant concepts and related work. Chapter 3 states requirements and design constraints. Chapter 4 explains the architecture and implementation. Chapter 5 documents the model and data workflow. Chapter 6 presents testing and validation. Chapter 7 provides evaluation placeholders for the team’s measured outputs. The final chapter gives conclusions and future work.','The appendices contain editable evidence templates. Replace every bracketed placeholder with verified project material before submission.')

add('CHAPTER 2 BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK','Clinical decision-support systems assist users by arranging information and offering evidence-linked prompts, but clinicians retain responsibility for interpretation and action. In machine-learning screening systems, performance is affected by population, data quality, prevalence and the selection of predictors. A result trained or benchmarked on one dataset may not generalize to another care setting.','For this reason, the platform treats each disease as a separate pipeline with its own features and applies an insufficient-data status when the relevant evidence is unavailable. This design is more defensible than forcing all conditions through a single generic score.')
add('2.1 MACHINE LEARNING FOR CLINICAL RISK','Supervised classifiers learn relationships between input features and labels in a reference dataset. In this project, a probability-like score is interpreted as a screening-risk estimate only. Its value depends on the quality, representativeness and preprocessing of the data used to construct the pipeline.','A useful clinical interface should show both the score category and the input factors that require attention. It should also avoid language such as “you have the disease.” A safe result screen instead advises that a qualified professional should evaluate symptoms, history, examination findings and confirmatory tests.')
add('2.2 EXPLAINABILITY AND HUMAN OVERSIGHT','Explainability is useful when it helps a clinician inspect why a score may be elevated, identify missing inputs and challenge implausible values. It does not itself establish validity. TwinHealth AI provides interpretable factors generated by the disease-specific adapters and reserves the decision to diagnose, treat or refer for a clinician.','Human oversight is also needed for activity recommendations. A care-plan draft must be approved and tailored for contraindications, medication interactions, renal status, pregnancy, acute illness and other factors that an academic prototype may not fully represent.')
add('2.3 LOCAL FIRST ARCHITECTURE','A local-first prototype minimizes dependence on third-party cloud services in a classroom demonstration. The currently implemented project uses local machine-learning pipelines and local JSON files for persisted records. This supports repeatable demonstration when a network connection or external API is unavailable.','Local storage is not a complete security solution. A production clinical system would require access control, encryption, audit trails, retention rules, consent processes, backups, vulnerability management and formal governance.')
add('2.4 RELATED WORK COMPARISON','Prior literature describes the promise of machine learning for prediction, as well as risks of bias, poor transportability and automation bias. The project is positioned as a workflow prototype that makes its limits visible rather than claiming clinical deployment readiness.',table=(['Area','Common approach','TwinHealth AI position'],[['Risk modelling','Single-condition classifier','Five separate disease adapters'],['Data handling','Assume all inputs exist','Reject or mark insufficient data'],['Result display','Score only','Score category plus factors and disclaimer'],['Care planning','Generic advice','Reviewable clinician draft and checklist'],['Deployment','Cloud or enterprise service','Local academic prototype']],[3.5,5.5,6]))
add('2.5 ETHICAL AND SAFETY CONSIDERATIONS','Health-related software must avoid overstating precision or authority. The interface should identify itself as a decision-support tool, document model boundaries and prompt clinical testing for diagnosis. It should not be used to delay urgent care. The test and demonstration environment must not use real protected health information.','Bias review is also essential. Differences in demographic representation, missing data, label quality and clinical access can affect model performance. Evaluation should report subgroup limitations where authorised data and sample sizes permit.')
add('2.6 RESEARCH GAP','Many student prototypes focus on a single prediction page without considering how data are persisted, reviewed by a clinician, communicated to a patient and followed through a measurable plan. This project addresses that integration gap at prototype scale.','The remaining gap is clinical validation. A prototype can demonstrate technical integration and usability, but prospective validation and governance are required before any clinical deployment.')

add('CHAPTER 3 REQUIREMENTS ANALYSIS AND DESIGN','Requirements were defined around two user roles: a clinician who records an assessment and reviews an output, and a patient who views an approved activity plan and records completion. The design also requires an administrator or project team member to configure model artifacts and maintain a development environment.','The requirements prioritize clarity, safe risk language and recoverable local persistence over broad feature expansion.')
add('3.1 FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS','The system shall provide a landing page, role selection, clinician intake, multi-disease risk analysis, a result view, a patient activity page and a clinician activity-monitoring page. Each assessment shall be linked to the relevant patient identifier within the local demo data.','The system shall validate required inputs, retain outputs and avoid presenting a normal-looking numeric estimate when a disease pipeline lacks sufficient required information.',table=(['ID','Requirement','Verification method'],[['FR1','Accept clinician-entered demographic, vital and laboratory inputs','Manual form test'],['FR2','Run five disease-specific analyses','API and result-page test'],['FR3','Persist assessment and care-plan data','Storage test'],['FR4','Show patient checklist by date','Patient-flow test'],['FR5','Show adherence to clinician','Cross-view activity test']],[2,10,3]))
add('3.2 NON FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS','The system should be understandable, responsive on common browsers, maintainable through separated backend modules and robust against malformed input. It should provide descriptive failure states rather than silent errors. It should use a consistent visual hierarchy so that disclaimers and insufficient-data notices are not hidden.','For this academic prototype, security requirements are documented as limitations rather than asserted as production controls. Email-only demo login is not a substitute for password authentication, authorization or regulated identity management.',table=(['Category','Requirement'],[['Usability','Users can identify next actions and status'],['Reliability','Writes use a lock and atomic replacement'],['Performance','Local inference completes within an acceptable demonstration delay'],['Maintainability','Routes, adapters and providers are separated'],['Safety','No diagnosis claim; missing evidence is visible']],[4,12]))
add('3.3 USE CASES','The primary clinician use case begins when the clinician selects the role, enters a patient identifier and records data. The system validates fields, runs eligible model pipelines and stores the assessment. The clinician reviews the risk report, considers missing evidence and, when appropriate, creates or reviews a care-plan draft.','The primary patient use case begins when the patient accesses the companion page using the demonstration identity. The patient reviews scheduled activities and records completion. The clinician may later refresh the activity view to inspect adherence information.')
add('3.4 DATA REQUIREMENTS','The application stores demo identities, patient records, assessments, care plans and activity-completion events. Only data needed for the prototype workflow should be collected. During a demonstration, the team must use synthetic or authorised de-identified values.','Each stored item should be traceable through a generated identifier and timestamp. Input units and source should be recorded in a final study or clinical evaluation, even if the classroom version represents them as form fields.')
add('3.5 RISK MANAGEMENT','Risks include incomplete input, inappropriate reliance on a score, model mismatch, inconsistent units, local file corruption and misuse of demo identity. Mitigations include feature sufficiency rules, explicit disclaimers, input validation, atomic persistence and limitations documentation.',table=(['Risk','Mitigation','Owner'],[['Missing laboratory value','Return insufficient-data status','System'],['User interprets output as diagnosis','Disclaimer and clinician review','Project team'],['Incorrect units','Form hints and validation','Clinician user'],['Concurrent write','Lock and atomic file replace','Backend'],['Unauthorised real data','Demo-data policy and training','Project team']],[4,8,4]))
add('3.6 FEASIBILITY STUDY','Technical feasibility is supported by a Python FastAPI backend, browser frontend and scikit-learn model pipelines that can execute on a local development machine. Operational feasibility is demonstrated through role-specific pages and persistence across the assessment-to-activity workflow.','Economic and schedule feasibility for the academic submission depend on available computing, data access and evaluation time. Enter the actual project cost, hardware specification, schedule variance and resources here after completion: [PUT FEASIBILITY EVIDENCE HERE].')

add('CHAPTER 4 SYSTEM DESIGN AND IMPLEMENTATION','TwinHealth AI follows a layered web-application structure. Browser pages gather and display information; FastAPI routes coordinate requests; adapters transform a common patient representation into disease-specific features; providers run model inference; and persistence services retain assessment, care-plan and activity records.','This separation improves testability because each adapter and service can be evaluated independently before an end-to-end browser flow is tested.',figure=True)
add('4.1 SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE','The frontend contains pages for landing, login, clinician intake, results, patient activity, clinician activity and clinician queue views. The backend serves these pages and exposes application routes. The prediction service orchestrates adapters for diabetes, heart disease, CKD, liver disease and stroke.','Model artifacts are executed locally in the configured provider path. JSON persistence is used for selected application records, with an optional SQLite initialization path present in the backend. The report should be updated if the final submitted build differs from this architecture.')
add('4.2 CLINICIAN WORKFLOW','A clinician enters a patient email identifier and relevant values in the assessment form. The backend validates input and passes a structured patient object to the prediction service. Each adapter determines whether the disease pipeline has enough features and normalizes values for the model.','The results page displays the assessment-specific output. The clinician must interpret the result within the patient’s context and may prepare a plan for review. The user interface should direct the clinician to clinical testing for diagnosis and to urgent services where appropriate.')
add('4.3 PATIENT WORKFLOW','The patient view organizes activities by date and time period. It is designed to show a plan created or approved in the clinical workflow and to let the patient mark items as completed. Completion events are saved with a task identifier, date and timestamp.','The patient view is a support interface. It must not instruct a patient to start, stop or alter medication without an attending clinician’s direction. Final submission screenshots should use fictional data only.')
add('4.4 PERSISTENCE DESIGN','The local JSON persistence engine stores users, patients, assessments, care plans and activity completions as separate files. To lower the chance of corruption, writes are serialized by an in-process lock, written to a temporary file and atomically replaced.','This is suitable for a classroom prototype but has constraints: it does not offer multi-host transactional consistency, database access control or enterprise audit guarantees. A production pathway would use an authenticated database and structured audit records.',table=(['Data file','Purpose','Example key'],[['users.json','Demo roles and identities','email'],['patients.json','Patient profile','patient id or email'],['assessments.json','Input and result snapshot','assessment id'],['care_plans.json','Clinician-reviewed plan','plan id'],['activity_completions.json','Completed task events','task/date']],[4,7,5]))
add('4.5 API AND ROUTE DESIGN','The application provides browser routes for the landing page, login, intake form, results page, patient dashboard and clinician activity view. It also exposes a health route and route groups for detection, clinical, model, authentication and activity operations.','The final report should add a route table generated from the exact version submitted. Record any added, removed or renamed endpoints here: [PUT FINAL API ROUTE EVIDENCE HERE].')
add('4.6 USER INTERFACE DESIGN','The interface design uses separate screens to reduce information overload. The intake form focuses on data capture. The results screen groups disease outputs and action links. The activity screens focus on planned tasks and recorded completion.','Safety-critical information should be visually prominent: data-insufficiency states, model limitations and the statement that clinical testing is required for diagnosis. Colour must not be the sole carrier of a status; text labels should remain visible.')
add('4.7 IMPLEMENTATION TECHNOLOGIES','The backend is implemented in Python using FastAPI. Local machine-learning artifacts use scikit-learn-compatible pipelines. The frontend uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Automated tests use pytest. The development project includes a Windows startup script and dependency file.','Document exact versions from the final environment here: Python [PUT VERSION], FastAPI [PUT VERSION], scikit-learn [PUT VERSION], browser [PUT VERSION], operating system [PUT VERSION].')

add('CHAPTER 5 MACHINE LEARNING AND DATA PIPELINE','The machine-learning component is organized as independent disease adapters rather than one universal classifier. Each adapter maps the common patient schema into the feature names, categories and units expected by its trained pipeline. The service records whether enough evidence was present before attempting inference.','This arrangement recognizes that the available predictors and intended output vary across conditions. A result from one model should not be treated as evidence for another condition.')
add('5.1 DATA PREPARATION','Before inference, input values are checked for presence, type and clinically meaningful representation as implemented by the application. Training pipelines may include imputation, scaling, categorical encoding or other transformations. The final project report must state the exact preprocessing performed during model training and the source dataset license or permission status.','No clinical claim should be made from a benchmark metric alone. The team should report the train/test split, validation procedure, class distribution, threshold choice and any calibration method used in the submitted build.')
add('5.2 DISEASE SPECIFIC MODELS','The project integrates models for diabetes, heart disease, CKD, liver disease and stroke risk. Each has different clinical variables and data quality considerations. In particular, CKD features can be closely related to diagnostic laboratory measurements, and stroke data may be imbalanced; both require cautious interpretation.','The implemented local pipelines use common supervised learning approaches such as logistic regression, random forest, extra trees and gradient boosting, as configured in the project. The final report should identify the artifact version and exact hyperparameters from the submitted codebase.',table=(['Risk area','Typical pipeline type','Important limitation'],[['Diabetes','Logistic regression pipeline','Requires appropriate metabolic measures'],['Heart disease','Random forest pipeline','Dataset/population transportability'],['CKD','Extra trees pipeline','Lab-feature leakage/collinearity risk'],['Liver disease','Gradient boosting pipeline','Moderate discrimination may vary'],['Stroke','Logistic regression pipeline','Class imbalance and calibration']],[3.5,5,6.5]))
add('5.3 FEATURE SUFFICIENCY CHECK','A central safety control is the sufficiency check. The prediction service first identifies present patient fields. For a disease whose critical requirements are absent, the service returns a clear insufficient-data outcome instead of predicting from weak substitutes.','This policy makes uncertainty visible to the clinician and supports a follow-up request for laboratory testing or history information. It does not guarantee that all remaining input values are accurate or clinically appropriate; normal clinical review is still required.')
add('5.4 EXPLAINABLE RISK REPORTING','The result representation includes disease name, probability or percentage where eligible, risk label and factors. Factors are intended to help a reviewer recognize which inputs contributed to the model context. They are not causal proof and should not be interpreted as a personalized treatment order.','The final user interface should use wording such as “risk estimate,” “screening result” and “requires clinical review.” It should not state that the model has confirmed disease.')
add('5.5 MODEL EVALUATION PROTOCOL','Evaluation should use a held-out test set or a documented cross-validation strategy. Report discrimination metrics such as ROC-AUC with confidence intervals where possible, as well as sensitivity, specificity, precision, recall, calibration and confusion matrices at the selected operating threshold.','Where the dataset permits, compare results across meaningful demographic groups and document sample-size limitations. A threshold should be justified by the intended screening use and should not be optimized solely for an attractive headline metric.')
add('5.6 RESULTS ENTRY TEMPLATE','Enter only measured, reproducible results in this section after executing the approved evaluation notebook or training script. Do not copy dataset-card or README benchmark figures into a final claim unless the team reproduced them using the stated protocol.',table=(['Model','ROC AUC','Sensitivity','Specificity','Test sample size'],[['Diabetes','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT N]'],['Heart disease','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT N]'],['CKD','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT N]'],['Liver disease','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT N]'],['Stroke','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT VALUE]','[PUT N]']],[3.5,2.5,3,3,3]))
add('5.7 LIMITATIONS OF MODEL OUTPUT','Model output can be affected by training-data bias, missing predictors, unit mismatch, label uncertainty, threshold choice, changes in disease prevalence and workflow context. Users may also over-trust a numerical output. These limitations make it inappropriate to use the prototype as an autonomous diagnosis or treatment system.','The system should support, not replace, history taking, physical examination, laboratory confirmation, clinician judgment and local care pathways.')

add('CHAPTER 6 TESTING AND VALIDATION','Testing covers component-level behavior, persistence, prediction orchestration and end-to-end role flows. The repository includes automated tests for adapters, local models, JSON storage, patient inputs, care plans, navigation, medication-related workflows and regression scenarios.','The exact number of passing tests, command output, code revision and execution environment must be inserted from the final test run. This report does not assume that a previous test total remains current.')
add('6.1 TEST STRATEGY','Unit tests verify individual adapters and utility behavior. Integration tests verify that route and service layers exchange the expected structures. End-to-end tests verify that a clinician assessment can lead to results and that patient activity becomes visible in a clinician view.','Manual usability testing should complement automated tests because it can reveal unclear wording, hidden controls or unsafe interpretation cues that a code-level assertion cannot detect.')
add('6.2 FUNCTIONAL TEST CASES','The following table is a submission template. Replace status and evidence with actual observed results, test dates and screenshots from the final build.',table=(['Test ID','Scenario','Expected result','Actual status'],[['TC01','Submit complete clinician form','Eligible disease outputs are shown','[PUT PASS/FAIL]'],['TC02','Omit required CKD input','Insufficient-data state is shown','[PUT PASS/FAIL]'],['TC03','Save care plan','Plan is linked to assessment','[PUT PASS/FAIL]'],['TC04','Complete patient activity','Completion persists by date','[PUT PASS/FAIL]'],['TC05','Refresh clinician activity','Adherence reflects completion','[PUT PASS/FAIL]']],[2,5,5,3]))
add('6.3 INPUT VALIDATION TESTING','Input validation tests should include blank values, non-numeric values, boundary values, invalid email formats, unexpected categorical labels and inconsistent units. Tests must verify that errors are comprehensible and that no unsafe numerical result is emitted when critical evidence is absent.','Add the final boundary-test evidence here: [PUT INPUT VALIDATION SCREENSHOTS OR TEST LOG REFERENCE HERE].')
add('6.4 PERSISTENCE AND CONCURRENCY TESTING','The local persistence approach uses an in-process lock and atomic replacement to reduce the likelihood of partial-file corruption. Tests should create, read and update records, then verify that independent files retain valid JSON. A concurrency scenario can simulate multiple completion updates or assessment writes.','This mechanism is limited to the prototype’s local process model. It does not replace a transaction-capable shared database for multi-server deployment.')
add('6.5 USABILITY VALIDATION','A small usability evaluation can ask participants to complete a clinician assessment, interpret an insufficient-data message and record a patient activity. Observe task completion, errors, time, wording confusion and suggestions. Participants must not be led to believe that the prototype gives medical advice.','Enter approved study details and consent status here: [PUT USER STUDY / USABILITY EVIDENCE HERE].')
add('6.6 SECURITY AND PRIVACY REVIEW','The academic application uses an email-based demo identity mechanism. It does not claim HIPAA, SOC 2, password authentication, encryption-at-rest or production authorization. Demonstrations must avoid real PHI.','A production implementation would require strong authentication, role-based authorization, encryption in transit and at rest, immutable audit logs, secure secrets management, monitoring, incident response and legal/ethical governance.')
add('6.7 DEPLOYMENT VALIDATION','Before a project demonstration, verify environment variables, model artifacts, dependency installation, application routes, database or JSON initialization and browser access. Demonstrate a health endpoint and at least one test run where permitted.','Deployment evidence: [PUT SCREENSHOT OF HEALTH ENDPOINT, STARTUP LOG, AND FINAL TEST COMMAND OUTPUT HERE].')

add('CHAPTER 7 EVALUATION AND DISCUSSION','Evaluation should distinguish software verification from clinical validity. A successful automated test confirms that the implementation behaved as expected for a specified scenario. It does not establish that a model is clinically safe or effective in a new population.','The project’s strongest evidence should therefore be reported in layers: software tests, data/model evaluation, usability observations and stated limitations. Each result must identify its data source, procedure and date.')
add('7.1 PLANNED OUTPUTS VERSUS ACHIEVED OUTPUTS','Use this table after final review to compare planned deliverables with observed evidence. Keep unresolved items visible rather than rewriting them as completed.',table=(['Planned output','Evidence','Status','Notes'],[['Local multi-disease assessment','[PUT API/SCREENSHOT]','[PUT STATUS]','[PUT NOTE]'],['Risk explanation display','[PUT SCREENSHOT]','[PUT STATUS]','[PUT NOTE]'],['Patient activity tracking','[PUT SCREENSHOT]','[PUT STATUS]','[PUT NOTE]'],['Automated test suite','[PUT LOG]','[PUT STATUS]','[PUT NOTE]'],['Model evaluation','[PUT METRIC TABLE]','[PUT STATUS]','[PUT NOTE]']],[4,4,3,4]))
add('7.2 PERFORMANCE RESULTS','System performance should be measured on the target machine under stated conditions. Useful measures include server startup time, time to submit a typical assessment, local inference latency, storage read/write time and page response time. Report repeated measurements or ranges rather than a single favourable run.','Performance table: [PUT HARDWARE, TEST DATE, METHOD, SAMPLE COUNT AND MEASURED LATENCY VALUES HERE].')
add('7.3 DISCUSSION OF RESULTS','The expected technical benefit of the system is workflow integration: a clinician can move from intake to risk summary, then to a reviewable plan and patient activity follow-up. The sufficiency check provides a more appropriate response to missing information than a forced score. Local execution supports a repeatable classroom demonstration.','These benefits do not demonstrate clinical effectiveness. The limitations around data representativeness, model validation, safety governance and security remain material and should be retained in the final discussion.')
add('7.4 FAILURE ANALYSIS','Failure analysis should record invalid inputs, unavailable model artifacts, unsupported feature combinations, network-free operation, storage errors and unclear user interactions. For each, record trigger, observed behavior, expected behavior, fix and regression-test reference.','Failure analysis evidence: [PUT ISSUE LOG OR TABLE HERE].')
add('7.5 PROJECT MANAGEMENT REFLECTION','The team should document actual roles, milestones, task allocation, meeting frequency, repository contributions and risks encountered. Avoid claiming contribution from a member who did not perform it. Each individual submission should contain the same approved team list on the first two pages and the individual candidate’s name on the declaration and acknowledgement pages.','Team contribution table: [PUT MEMBER-WISE CONTRIBUTION HERE].')
add('7.6 DEMONSTRATION SCRIPT','A concise demonstration can begin with the landing page and the safety statement. Next, use a fictional patient identifier to complete a clinician assessment, show an eligible risk result and an insufficient-data outcome, then review a care-plan draft. Finally, use the patient page to record an activity and refresh the clinician activity view.','Do not enter real patient data during the demonstration. State clearly that model output is a risk estimate and clinical testing is required for diagnosis.')

add('CHAPTER 8 CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK','TwinHealth AI demonstrates a local web workflow for multi-disease risk assessment, clinician-oriented review and patient activity tracking. The system separates disease-specific model adapters, retains local assessment records and returns an insufficient-data status when required evidence is missing. The presentation of risk outputs is intentionally bounded: the platform supports assessment and discussion, while diagnosis requires clinical testing and qualified professional judgment.','The project’s implementation should be judged against its verified software behavior and documented model evaluation, not against unmeasured clinical impact. The final report must retain any failed tests, incomplete objectives and known limitations.')
add('8.1 FUTURE WORK','Future development could replace demo identity with standards-based authentication and authorization, migrate local files to an encrypted auditable database, add controlled data-import workflows, perform calibration and external validation, introduce clinician-configurable thresholds and support accessibility evaluation.','Additional work could include governance review, clinical expert evaluation, prospective usability study, privacy impact assessment and rigorous monitoring for performance drift. None of these should be represented as complete until evidence is available.')
add('8.2 FINAL SUBMISSION CHECKLIST','Before binding, replace all bracketed placeholders, update the table of contents and lists, verify team/member details, confirm certificate signatures, insert actual metrics and screenshots, check APA references, and run spelling and page-break review. Ensure that every individual submits their own copy and that the cover/title pages list all team members where applicable.','Final checklist status: [PUT COMPLETION DATE AND REVIEWER NAME HERE].')

